Given the matrix: \[P = \begin{bmatrix} 3 &amp; 1 \\ 1 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix}\] We are given a transformation mapping the vector \[\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}\] in set \(S\) to a new vector \[\begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix}\] via the relation: \[\begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix} = P \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}\] The target condition defines a unit circle in the transformed coordinate plane: \[a^2 + b^2 = 1\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>Expanding the matrix transformation explicitly into a system of linear algebraic equations: \[\begin{aligned}
    a &amp;= 3x + y \\
    b &amp;= x + 3y
\end{aligned}\] Substituting these algebraic expressions directly into the unit circle constraint equation (\(a^2 + b^2 = 1\)): \[(3x + y)^2 + (x + 3y)^2 = 1\] \[\left(9x^2 + 6xy + y^2\right) + \left(x^2 + 6xy + 9y^2\right) = 1\] \[10x^2 + 12xy + 10y^2 = 1\] This second-degree homogeneous polynomial represents a conic section. To analyze its primary geometric features, we examine the general equation form \(Ax^2 + Bxy + Cy^2 = 1\): \[A = 10, \quad B = 12, \quad C = 10\] Computing the discriminant to identify the specific conic geometry: \[B^2 - 4AC = (12)^2 - 4(10)(10) = 144 - 400 = -256 &lt; 0\] Since the discriminant is strictly negative and \(A = C\), the conic section represents a rotated ellipse. To find the orientation of its major and minor axes, we can evaluate the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the symmetric matrix associated with the quadratic form: \[Q = \begin{bmatrix} 10 &amp; 6 \\ 6 &amp; 10 \end{bmatrix}\] The characteristic equation is \(\det(Q - \lambda I) = 0\): \[(10 - \lambda)^2 - 36 = 0 \implies 10 - \lambda = \pm 6\] \[\begin{aligned}
    \lambda_1 = 16 &amp;\implies \text{associated with the minor axis (higher curvature)} \\
    \lambda_2 = 4 &amp;\implies \text{associated with the major axis (lower curvature)}
\end{aligned}\] Finding the eigenvector for the maximum eigenvalue \(\lambda_1 = 16\) to identify the direction of the minor axis: \[(Q - 16I)X = O \implies \begin{bmatrix} -6 &amp; 6 \\ 6 &amp; -6 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \implies -x_1 + x_2 = 0 \implies X_{\text{minor}} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\] Thus, the geometric locus of set \(S\) defines an ellipse with its minor axis oriented along the vector direction \[\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\].</div> \[\boxed{\text{(D) an ellipse with minor axis along $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>Let \[X = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}\] and \[Y = \begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix}\]. The given transformation is: \[Y = PX\] The equation of the unit circle \(a^2 + b^2 = 1\) can be expressed in vector-matrix layout as: \[Y^{\top}Y = 1\] Substituting \(Y = PX\) into this vector product: \[(PX)^{\top}(PX) = 1\] \[X^{\top} P^{\top} P X = 1\] Since \(P\) is a real symmetric matrix (\(P^{\top} = P\)), we compute the core matrix \(P^{\top}P = P^2\): \[P^2 = \begin{bmatrix} 3 &amp; 1 \\ 1 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 3 &amp; 1 \\ 1 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 9+1 &amp; 3+3 \\ 3+3 &amp; 1+9 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 10 &amp; 6 \\ 6 &amp; 10 \end{bmatrix}\] The transformed quadratic form equation becomes: \[X^{\top} \begin{bmatrix} 10 &amp; 6 \\ 6 &amp; 10 \end{bmatrix} X = 1\] The eigenvalues of this matrix determine the principal axes of the conic section: <ul><li><strong>Sum of Eigenvalues (\(\lambda_1 + \lambda_2\)):</strong> \(\text{Trace}(P^2) = 10 + 10 = 20\)</li><li><strong>Product of Eigenvalues (\(\lambda_1 \lambda_2\)):</strong> \(\det(P^2) = (10)(10) - (6)(6) = 100 - 36 = 64\)</li></ul> Solving the characteristic polynomial \(\lambda^2 - 20\lambda + 64 = 0 \implies (\lambda - 16)(\lambda - 4) = 0\), we find \(\lambda = 16\) and \(\lambda = 4\). Since both eigenvalues are positive and distinct (\(\lambda_1 \neq \lambda_2 &gt; 0\)), the equation represents an Ellipse. <ul><li>The major axis lies along the eigenvector of the smallest eigenvalue (\(\lambda = 4\)).</li><li>The minor axis lies along the eigenvector of the largest eigenvalue (\(\lambda = 16\)).</li></ul> Let us find the eigenvector for the minor axis (\(\lambda = 16\)): \[(P^2 - 16I)X = O \implies \begin{bmatrix} 10-16 &amp; 6 \\ 6 &amp; 10-16 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}\] \[\begin{bmatrix} -6 &amp; 6 \\ 6 &amp; -6 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \implies -6x + 6y = 0 \implies x = y\] This gives the clear directional vector configuration: \[X_{\text{minor}} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\]</div> \[\boxed{\text{(D) an ellipse with minor axis along $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Matrix Transformation of Fields:</strong> A spherical boundary \(\|Y\|^2 = 1\) under linear transformations shifts to an elliptical quadratic field described by \(X^{\top}(P^{\top}P)X = 1\).</li><li><strong>Spectral Association Rule:</strong> In a quadratic form \(X^{\top}MX = 1\), the principal axes match the eigenvectors of \(A\). The maximum eigenvalue (\(\lambda_{\max}\)) contracts the coordinate line the most, defining the direction of the minor axis.</li><li><strong>Symmetric Properties:</strong> If a matrix has identical diagonal elements (\(P_{11} = P_{22}\)), its eigenvectors are always symmetric reflections along the line \(x = y\) and \(x = -y\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li><strong>Multiplication Order:</strong> Remember \((PX)^{\top} = X^{\top}P^{\top}\). Reversing this order will result in non-conformable matrix dimensions.</li><li><strong>Minor vs Major Confusion:</strong> A common pitfall in competitive examinations is associating the larger eigenvalue with the major axis. Because the axis semi-length is proportional to \(1/\sqrt{\lambda}\), the larger eigenvalue produces a shorter axis (minor axis).</li></ul></div>

