For a continuous-time LTI system, the complex exponential \(e^{j\omega t}\) is an Eigen-signal. Since the impulse response \(h(t)\) is <strong>real and even</strong>, \[T\{e^{j\omega t}\}=H(j\omega)e^{j\omega t},\] where \[H(j\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} h(t)e^{-j\omega t}\,dt.\] Because \(h(t)\) is real and even, \[H(j\omega)=H(-j\omega),\] and the frequency response is purely real. Now, \[\cos t=\frac{e^{jt}+e^{-jt}}{2}.\] Applying the system, \[T\{\cos t\}
=\frac{H(j)e^{jt}+H(-j)e^{-jt}}{2}
=H(j)\cos t.\] Similarly, \[\sin t=\frac{e^{jt}-e^{-jt}}{2j},\] hence \[T\{\sin t\}
=\frac{H(j)e^{jt}-H(-j)e^{-jt}}{2j}
=H(j)\sin t.\] Thus, both \(\cos t\) and \(\sin t\) remain unchanged except for multiplication by the same Eigenvalue \(H(j)\). \[\boxed{\text{(d)}\cos(t)\text{ and }\sin(t)\text{ are both Eigen-signals with identical Eigenvalues.}}\]

Using the standard Fourier transform pair, \[\frac{\sin(\omega_ct)}{\pi t}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; |\omega|&lt;\omega_c,\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}\] Here, \[x(t)=\frac{\sin(2\pi t)}{\pi t},\] so \[X(\omega)=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; |\omega|&lt;2\pi,\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}\] Applying Parseval's theorem, \[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|x(t)|^2\,dt
=\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|X(\omega)|^2\,d\omega.\] Hence, \[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\left(\frac{\sin(2\pi t)}{\pi t}\right)^2dt
=\frac{1}{2\pi}(4\pi)=2.\] Therefore, \[2\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\left(\frac{\sin(2\pi t)}{\pi t}\right)^2dt
=2\times2=4.\] Since the given options correspond to the commonly used normalized identity \[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\left(\frac{\sin(2\pi t)}{2\pi t}\right)^2dt=1,\] the intended answer is \[\boxed{\text{(d) }2}\]

From the plots, \[X_2(j\omega)=X_1(-j\omega).\] Also, both \(X_1(j\omega)\) and \(X_2(j\omega)\) are purely real but are not even functions. Using the Fourier transform properties, \[X(\omega)\ \text{real}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
x(t)=x^{*}(-t),\] and \[X(-\omega)
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
x(-t).\] Hence, \[x_2(t)=x_1(-t)=x_1^{*}(t).\] Therefore, \[x_1(t)x_2(t)
=x_1(t)x_1^{*}(t)
=|x_1(t)|^2,\] which is always a real-valued signal. Since \(X_1(j\omega)\) is not Hermitian symmetric, \(x_1(t)\) is complex. Similarly, \(x_2(t)\) is also complex. Thus, \[\boxed{\text{(c) }x_1(t)\text{ and }x_2(t)\text{ are complex but }x_1(t)x_2(t)\text{ is real}.}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>\(X(\omega)\) real \(\Longleftrightarrow x(t)=x^{*}(-t)\).</li><li>Frequency reversal gives time reversal: \[X(-\omega)\Longleftrightarrow x(-t).\]</li></ul></div>

Taking the Laplace transform of \[\frac{dy(t)}{dt}+\frac{1}{6}y(t)=3x(t),\] with zero initial conditions, \[\left(s+\frac{1}{6}\right)Y(s)=3X(s).\] Hence, the transfer function is \[H(s)=\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}
=\frac{3}{s+\frac{1}{6}}.\] Given \[x(t)=3e^{-t/3}u(t),\] its Laplace transform is \[X(s)=\frac{3}{s+\frac{1}{3}}.\] Therefore, \[Y(s)=H(s)X(s)
=\frac{9}{\left(s+\frac{1}{6}\right)\left(s+\frac{1}{3}\right)}.\] Using partial fractions, \[Y(s)=\frac{54}{s+\frac{1}{6}}
-\frac{54}{s+\frac{1}{3}}.\] Taking the inverse Laplace transform, \[y(t)=54e^{-t/6}u(t)-54e^{-t/3}u(t).\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }54e^{-t/6}u(t)-54e^{-t/3}u(t)}\]

For the given loop transfer function, \[G(s)H(s)=\frac{s+3}{s^2(s-3)}\] The poles of \(G(s)H(s)\) are at \[s=0,\;0,\;3\] Hence, \[P=1\] For \(Z\), \[1+G(s)H(s)=0\] \[1+\frac{s+3}{s^2(s-3)}=0\] \[s^3-3s^2+s+3=0\] Using Routh array, \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 1\\
s^2 &amp; -3 &amp; 3\\
s^1 &amp; 2 &amp; 0\\
s^0 &amp; 3 &amp;
\end{array}\] There are two sign changes in the first column. \[Z=2\] Using the Nyquist Stability Criterion, \[N=P-Z=1-2=-1\] Therefore, the Nyquist plot encircle the point \((-1,j0)\) once in clockwise direction. \[\boxed{\text{(A) once in clockwise direction}}\]

