In Newton-Raphson load flow using polar coordinates: \[\text{Unknown voltage angles}
=
(n-1)\] where \(n\) is the total number of buses excluding the slack bus. Both PV and PQ buses contribute voltage angle unknowns. Hence, converting a PV bus to PQ bus does not affect the number of unknown voltage angles. However, for voltage magnitude unknowns: \[\text{Unknown voltage magnitudes}
=
\text{Number of PQ buses}\] At a PV bus, voltage magnitude is specified and therefore not treated as an unknown. When a PV bus is converted into a PQ bus due to reactive power limit violation, its voltage magnitude also becomes an unknown. Since two PV buses are converted to PQ buses, \[\text{Number of voltage magnitude unknowns increases by }2\] while \[\text{Number of voltage angle unknowns remains unchanged}\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{\text{(B)}}\] \[\text{The number of unknown voltage angles remains unchanged and the number of unknown voltage magnitudes increases by 2.}\]

