For a lossless transmission line, the surge impedance is \[Z_n = \sqrt{\frac{L}{C}}\] Given, \[L = 1.6 \times 10^{-3}\,\text{H/km}\] \[C = 10 \times 10^{-9}\,\text{F/km}\] Therefore, \[Z_n
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1.6 \times 10^{-3}}
{10 \times 10^{-9}}
}\] \[Z_n = 400\,\Omega\] The Surge Impedance Loading (SIL) is \[\text{SIL}
=
\frac{V_{LL}^2}{Z_n}\] \[=
\frac{400 \times 400}{400}
=
400\,\text{MW}\] Since the line is delivering \[300\,\text{MW} &lt; 400\,\text{MW}\] the loading is less than SIL. Hence, the line behaves capacitively and produces excess reactive power. To maintain the receiving-end voltage at the desired value, inductive shunt compensation is required to absorb the excess reactive power. \[\boxed{\text{(B) Inductive}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Surge impedance: \[Z_n = \sqrt{\frac{L}{C}}\]</li><li>Surge impedance loading: \[\text{SIL} = \frac{V_{LL}^2}{Z_n}\]</li><li>If load \(&lt;\) SIL, the transmission line behaves capacitively.</li><li>If load \(&gt;\) SIL, the transmission line behaves inductively.</li></ul></div>

